## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
Lithium-sulfur batteries   
Metal-organic frameworks   
Molybdenum carbide   
Lithium polysulfides immobilization   
Electrocatalytic effect
The shuttle effect causing by the dissolution of lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) and sluggish redox reaction kinetics during the charge/discharge processes significantly reduce the cycle life and sulfur utilization in lithium-sulfur (Li S) batteries. Herein, $\mathrm{Mo_{2}C-C}$ nano-octahedrons $(\mathrm{Mo_{2}C-C}$ NOs) with $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ nanoparticles embedded in a three-dimensional porous carbon matrix have been prepared via a metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) assisted strategy, and used as the sulfur host for Li S batteries. The $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathode demonstrates a high initial specific capacity of 1396 and $1050\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ at $_{0.1\mathrm{~C~}}$ and ${\textsc{1C}}_{:}$ respectively, with an ultra-low average specific capacity decay rate of $0.0457\%$ per cycle within 600 cycles at $_{1\mathrm{~C~}}$ Moreover, $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ cathode with high areal sulfur loading of $4.2\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{}S}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ can deliver a high initial discharge specific capacity of $807\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ , and a capacity of $623\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{C}$ . The existence of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ nanoparticles can not only immobilize LiPSs via the formation of Mo S bond, but also electrocatalytically accelerate the redox kinetics towards LiPSs conversion. Meanwhile, the interconnected porous carbon matrices contributes to effective sulfur/electrolyte infiltration, fast electron $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\mathbf{Li}^{+}$ ion transportation and sufficient buffer for volume change.
## 1. Introduction
With the rapid development of portable electronics, electrical vehicles (EVs)/hybrid electrical vehicles (HEVs) and large-scale energy storage devices, the requirements of new energy storage systems with high-energy density are ever-increasing, which is difficult to satisfy by the conventional lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) [1 3]. In this regard, endowed by the multi-electron redox reaction between sulfur and lithium, the theoretical energy density and specific capacity of sulfur cathode reach 2500 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ and $1675\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, which make lithium-sulfur (Li S) batteries the most promising alternative to the conventional LIBs [4,5]. Unfortunately, the commercial application of Li S batteries is greatly impeded by the notorious shuttle effect causing by soluble lithium polysulfides (LiPSs) intermediates, significant volume change during the charge/discharge processes and sluggish kinetics of the LiPSs redox reactions [6,7].
To overcome the intrinsic deficiencies in sulfur cathode, massive efforts have been exerted to combine sulfur with certain host materials to afford the composite cathode [8 10]. Among the promising options, carbonaceous materials with favorable conductivity and high specific area show great potential, such as the porous carbons [11 13], graphene [14], carbon nanotubes (CNTs) [15], etc. Generally, the S C composite cathodes can improve the S utilization and rate performance of Li S batteries [16]. However, suffering from the weak interaction between the polar LiPSs and nonpolar carbonaceous materials, the diffusion of LiPSs can only be inadequately inhibited by physical confinement rather than chemical adsorption, thus resulting in serious shuttle effect during the long term cycling processes [17]. To effectively immobilize LiPSs, polar transition metal compounds, such as $\mathrm{{MnO}}_{2}$ [18], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [19], $\mathrm{VS}_{2}$ [20], TiN [21], NbC [22], etc., have been used as the sulfur host materials for Li S batteries. Different from the conventional carbon materials, the transition metal compound can effectively immobilize LiPSs via the chemical interaction, which is critical for the suppression of the shuttle effect [23]. Furthermore, recent researches have revealed that the electrocatalytic effect of transition metal compounds can greatly accelerate the kinetics of the LiPSs redox processes, which contribute to higher sulfur utilization and longer cycling life of Li S batteries [24,25]. However, the limited specific surface area and conductivity of the aforementioned transition metal compound materials restrict the sulfur loading mass and rate performance [26]. Overall, carbonaceous materials and transition metal compounds have their own advantages and disadvantages when used as the sulfur host. Therefore, combining the polar transition metal compounds with electrocatalytic ability with the conductive carbon matrix to afford the muti-functional sulfur host is a promising solution to tackle the above deficiencies in Li S batteries.
Transition metal carbides, such as ${\sf W}_{2}{\sf C}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ (MXene) and $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ have captured intensive attentions in the field of catalysis and energy storage, benefiting from their Pt-like catalytic activity, favorable thermal stability, desirable conductivity and metallic characteristics [27 29]. In particular, $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ is reported to be a potential sulfur host material owing to its favorable conductivity, strong polarity and outstanding electrocatalytic ability [30]. However, controllable synthesis of nanostructured $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ -carbon hybrids with regular shape, porous structure and desirable polarity towards effective sulfur infiltration and LiPSs immobilization is still of great difficulty, due to the challenge of obtaining uniform carbonization and avoiding structure collapse of nanoparticles at the high calcining temperature [26,31]. Recently, porous transition metal compound-carbon hybrids derived from MOFs have attracted increasing interests [32 34]. Inheriting the porous structure and metallic ions/clusters of MOFs precursors, these hybrid materials have high surface areas, uniform porosity and abundant chemical active sites [35]. Therefore, when suitable Mo-based MOFs are chosen as precursor, such MOFs-assisted method allows for the synthesis of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ materials with regular and porous structure, high polarity and favorable electrocatalytic activity.
Herein, porous nano-octahedrons with ultrafine $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ nanocrystallites encapsulated in porous carbon substrate (denoted as $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs) have been successfully prepared via a MOFs-assisted strategy, and used as a sulfur host for Li S batteries. Initially, the carbon matrices of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs enabled effective sulfur/electrolyte infiltration, fast elec$\mathrm{{\tron/Li^{+}}}$ ion transportation and sufficient buffer for volume change. Moreover, the highly polar $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ can chemically adsorb the LiPSs, endowing by the formation of strong Mo S bond. Furthermore, the $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanoparticles with desirable electrocatalytic activity can boost the redox reaction kinetics of sulfur species. Consequently, the designed $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\mathsf{N O s@S}$ cathode exhibited outstanding electrochemical performance. With a high sulfur content of $72.15\mathrm{wt\%}$ , the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathode delivered a favorable specific capacity of $1396\mathrm{mAh}\ g^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~C~}$ and an ultra-low average specific capacity decay rate of $0.0457\%$ per cycle within 600 cycles at 1 C. Overall, the electrochemical test results show promising progress in the development of multifunctional sulfur host materials for high-performance Li S batteries.
## 2. Results and discussions
As illustrated in Fig. 1, porous $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs were prepared by a facile pyrolysis of $[\mathrm{Cu_{2}(B T C)_{4/3}(H_{2}O)_{2}}]_{6}[\mathrm{H_{3}P M o_{12}O_{40}}]$ (NENU-5; BTC $\c=$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) and subsequent etching treatment [26]. Initially, the NENU-5 nano-octahedrons (denoted as NENU-5 NOs) were prepared via a simple and scalable co-precipitation process at room temperature. As shown in the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) picture Fig. 2a, the NENU-5 NOs are with a smooth surface, octahedral shape and a size of about $800\mathrm{nm}$ . It is worth noting that the size and shape of the as-synthesized NENU-5 particles can be easily tuned by adjusting the addition of L-glutamic acid, which can effectively slow down the nucleation rate (Fig. S1). As shown in Fig. S2, the powder XRD pattern of as-synthesized NENU-5 is in agreement with that of simulated NENU-5, which suggests the favorable crystallinity and high phase purity [36].
Black $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs powder (Fig. S3c) was obtained by removing the metallic Cu with $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}$ aqueous solution $(0.1\mathbf{M})$ . SEM image (Fig. 2c) shows that the octahedral shape of the nanoparticles is well retained after the etching process. The XRD pattern of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs (Fig. 3a) shows only the diffraction peaks of ${\upbeta}{\mathrm{-Mo}}_{2}{\mathrm{C}}$ (PDF#35 0787), indicating complete removal of Cu. In general, the $_{\beta-\mathrm{Mo_{2}C}}$ exhibits a higher electrocatalytic activity than other molybdenum carbides (e.g., $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\alpha-Mo_{2}C}$ , $\scriptstyle\gamma-\mathbf{Mo_{2}C}$
G. Chen et al.
$\scriptstyle\mathtt{\eta_{\mathtt{l}}-M o_{2}C)}$ [28], which means that the as-prepared $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs might catalytically promote the redox conversion of LiPSs when used as the sulfur host material [39]. The ICP-OES analysis results show that the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ content in the as-prepared $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs is $48.69\mathrm{wt\%}$ (Table S1 shows more details). Besides, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs further reveals the composition and surface chemical state of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs. The survey spectrum of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs (Fig. 3b) shows well-distinguished signals at 531.4, 413.4, 395.3, 285.9 and $231.7\mathrm{eV}$ , which can be ascribed to O 1s, Mo $3{\tt p}_{1/2},$ Mo ${}_{3\mathrm{p}_{3/2},}$ C 1s and Mo 3d, respectively, further confirming the presence of molybdenum carbide [40]. The existence of O element may stem from the oxygen-containing groups of the carbon matrices or the slight oxidization of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ during the preparation process [39].
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was applied to further reveal the microstructure of the as-prepared $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}$ NOs. As shown in Fig. 2e, the $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}$ hybrids exhibit several rhombic sides of octahedral nanoparticles, consistent with the SEM image. Besides, the highresolution (HR) TEM image of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs (Fig. 2f) shows the structure of the ultrafine $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanoparticles (darker areas indicated by white oval dotted line) embedding in the amorphous carbon matrices. Moreover, a closer HRTEM image (Fig. 2g) clearly exhibits distinct lattice spacings of 0.23 and $0.18\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (101) and (102) lattice facets of the $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanocrystallites, respectively, which is further confirmed by selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern as shown in Fig. 2i. In addition, the TEM image and corresponding energydispersive spectrometry (EDS) elemental mappings of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs (Figs. S5a c) exhibit homogeneous distribution of Mo and C elements, indicating a successful incorporation of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ nanocrystallites and carbon substrate. Furthermore, the presence of amorphous carbon in the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs can be further confirmed by their Raman spectrum (Fig. 3c), where the characteristic D- and G-bands of the carbon at 1350 and $1594\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be clearly distinguished, with an $\mathrm{I_{G}/I_{D}}$ ratio of 0.943. On the other hand, according to the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) analysis results (Fig. S4c), after the etching process, the specific surface area and pore volume of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs increase to $196.{\overset{\cdot}{4}}6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $0.20\mathrm{m}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, owing–to the removal of Cu nanoparticles from the carbon matrices. Overall, the high specific surface area and abundant pore volume of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs can not only provide enough space for sulfur infiltration and buffer for the volume change, but also facilitate the interfacial interaction with LiPSs and free transfer of electron $/\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ion. For comparison, the SEM image (Fig. S6a) and XRD pattern (Fig. S6b) of commercial $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ bulks (Shanghai Yunfu Nanotechnology Co., Ltd) are also provided. As shown in Figs. S6a b, commercial $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ bulks are with smooth surface, irregular shape and large size, and the major XRD peaks are assigned to ${\upbeta}{\mathrm{-Mo}}_{2}{\mathrm{C}}$ $(\mathrm{PDF}\#35–0787)$ ).
Sublimed sulfur was infiltrated into the as-prepared $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs via a melt-diffusion method to afford the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ composites [41]. The XRD pattern of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ shows diffraction peaks of $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ $(\mathrm{PDF}\#35–0787)$ and sulfur $(\mathrm{PDF}\#08–0247)$ ), indicating successful preparation of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ composites. As demonstrated in Fig. 2d, the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ composites are still in pristine octahedral shape, and there are no obvious sulfur particles on the surface of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs. Moreover, the TEM image (Fig. 2h) shows that the $\mathrm{Mo_{2}C-C}$ NOs are filled with numerous black particles after the sulfur infiltration process, and the EDS elemental mappings (Fig. 2j-m) further reveals the homogeneous distributions of C, Mo and S elements, suggesting that S was uniformly dispersed into the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs. Furthermore, it is worth noting that the specific surface area $(30.03\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and the pore volume $(0.02{\overset{-}{3}}\mathbf{m}^{3}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ composite are dramatically lower than those of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs, further confirming an effective sulfur infiltration (Fig. S4d). In addition, Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@8$ and carbon nano-tubes $@s$ $(\mathrm{CNTs}@S)$ composites were obtained in similar melt-diffusion processes and used as comparisons. As determined by the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) analysis (Fig. 3d), the sulfur content in the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ , commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and $\mathbf{CNTs}@s$ composite are 72.15, 73.54 and $68.73\mathrm{wt\%}$ , respectively.
To evaluate the advantages of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs as an advanced sulfur host for Li S batteries, half-cells using the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @S$ , Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and CNTs $@s$ electrodes as cathodes and lithium metal as anodes were assembled. Fig. 4a exhibits the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves of the cell with the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O}s@\mathtt{S}$ cathode for 4 cycles at a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ within the potential window of $1.7{-}2.8\mathrm{V}$ versus $\mathrm{{Li/Li^{+}}}$ . In the cathodic scan process, two sharp cathodic peaks at 2.32 and $2.04\mathrm{V}$ can be clearly observed, which can be ascribed to the reduction of elemental sulfur to high-order lithium LiPSs $(\mathrm{Li_{2}S_{n}}.$ $4<\mathtt{n}<8)$ and their further conversion towards solid-state discharged products $\mathrm{Li_{2}S_{2}}/\mathrm{Li_{2}}S$ , respectively. In subsequent anodic scan, the typical anodic peak occurring at $2.37{-}2.41\mathrm{V}$ is associated with the reversed transformation from $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ to $s_{8}$ [42]. In particular, different from irregular peaks intensity variation and position shift in the CV profiles of the Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and $\mathrm{CNTs}@S$ cathodes (Figs. S7a b), the CV curves of the $\mathtt{M o_{2}C-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @S$ cathode are almost overlapped in the subsequent cycles, indicating a favorable cycling stability and reversibility [43]. Moreover, as demonstrated in Fig. 4b, the initial CV curves of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{NOs@}}\varnothing s$ and
Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@\mathbf{S}$ cathodes show higher current density, certain positive shift in the cathodic peaks and negative shift in the anodic peaks when compared with those of the $\mathrm{CNTs}@S$ cathode, suggesting a decrease in polarization [30]. Such result can be attributed to the electrocatalytic effect of $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ on the redox process of the Li S battery. On the other hand, the catalytic effect of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ is further confirmed by the polarization curves of a $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}{\mathrm{-Li}_{2}S_{6}}$ symmetric cell recorded within a voltage range of $-1.0$ to $+1.0\mathrm{V}$ with a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . As shown in Fig. 4c, the polarization curves of the cells with the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs and Commercial $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ electrodes containing the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ catholyte exhibit obvious current response and distinct redox peaks at $0.15\mathrm{V}$ and $-0.15\mathrm{V}$ , suggesting favorable electrocatalytic ability of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ on the conversion between $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ and sulfur [44]. In comparison, cell without $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ catholyte shows negligible current response, while cell with the CNTs electrode containing the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ catholyte shows only weak current response during the scan process.
Corresponding to the CV profiles, the charge/discharge voltage profiles of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @S$ , Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and $\mathrm{CNTs}@S$ cathodes at a rate of $0.1\mathrm{{C}}$ (Fig. 4d) shows two typical discharge voltage plateaus for Li S batteries [45]. As expected, benefiting from the outstanding electrocatalytic ability and conductivity of the $\mathrm{{Mo}_{2}C-C}$ NOs, the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\mathsf{N O s@S}$ cathode shows the smallest polarization potential and the highest initial discharge/charge capacities of $1396/1344\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in comparison with the counterparts, with a high initial Coulombic efficiency of $96.28\%$ . The improved electrochemical kinetics of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathode can be further revealed by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements. As shown in Fig. 4e, the Nyquist plots of the tested fresh cathodes are all consisted of a depressed semicircle at medium frequency domain (charge-transfer resistance) and a straight tail at low frequency (Warburg impedance) [46]. It can be clearly observed from Fig. 4e that the diameter of the semicircle for the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathode in the medium frequency region is smaller than those of the Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and $\mathbf{CNTs}@s$ cathodes, indicating that the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\mathsf{N O s@S}$ cathode possesses a lower charge transfer resistance.
Generally, the enhanced reaction kinetics of cathode can greatly promote the rate performances of Li S batteries [47]. which can be confirmed by the electrochemical tests of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @S$ , Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and $\mathbf{CNTs}@s$ cathodes under different rates. As shown in Fig. 4f, upon the application of various current rates of 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, $_{5.0\mathrm{~C~}}$ , the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\mathsf{N O s@S}$ cathode deliver the specific capacities of 1398, 1219, 1100, 979, 812, 623, 494, 395 and 337 mAh ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , respectively, which are generally higher than those of Commercial $\mathbf{Mo_{2}C}@S$ and $\mathrm{CNTs}@S$ cathodes. Subsequently, when the current rate is switched back to $0.5\mathrm{C}$ , the specific capacity of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{NOs@}}$ cathode still achieve a highly reversible specific capacity of 924 mAh $\ensuremath{\boldsymbol{{g}}}^{-1}$ , exhibiting high sulfur utilization and outstanding rate performances. Moreover, the long-term cycling performance of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O}s@\mathtt{S}$ cathode at 1 C is better than those of the other cathodes. As showed in Fig. $4g_{;}$ , benefiting from the effective suppression on shuttle effect and electrocatalytic ability of polar $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanoparticles, the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{N O s@S}}$ cathode achieves a high initial discharge specific capacity of $1050\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ and maintains a capacity of $762\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ after 600 cycles at $^{1\mathrm{~C~}}$ , corresponding to an average specific capacity decay rate of merely $0.0457\%$ per cycle. Notably, the Coulombic efficiency of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ cathode keeps at more than $99\%$ during the cycling process, indicating desirable cycle stability. In comparison, after 600 cycles, the Commercial $\mathbf{Mo_{2}C}@S$ and $\mathbf{CNTs}@s$ cathodes obtain average specific capacity decay rate of $0.101\%$ and $0.143\%$ , respectively. In addition, the shape and structure of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs@S can be well retained after 600 cycles at 1C (Fig. S8), further confirming the structural stability of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs.
Furthermore, since the cycling performance of Li S batteries with high sulfur loading is critical for their practical applications, $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}$ $\mathsf{N O s@S}$ cathodes with areal sulfur loading of 2.1, 3.2 and $4.2\mathrm{mgs}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ are tested at the rate of $0.5\mathrm{C}$ to display their favorable electrochemical performances. Fig. 4h shows that the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathodes with the areal sulfur loading of 2.1, 3.2 and $4.2~\mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{S}}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ obtain a high initial discharge specific capacity of 990, 914 and $807\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ , respectively, and maintains a capacity of 835, 686 and $623\mathrm{mAh~g^{-1}}$ , respectively, after 100 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{~C~}$ . Such desirable cycling performances exhibit promising progress towards practical applications of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathodes. Overall, the above outstanding electrochemical performances of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathodes can be attributed to the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs, whose carbon matrices and $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanoparticles enable the fast electron/ $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ion transportation, effective electrochemical catalysis on LiPSs redox reaction and sufficient suppression on the shuttle effect. It is worth noting that the electrochemical performance of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s@S}$ cathodes are comparable with those reported in state-of-the-art composite sulfur cathodes (Table S2 shows more details).
Visual LiPSs absorption ability test, ex situ UV vis spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy analyses were conducted to further probe the chemical interaction between different sulfur host materials (e.i., $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}$ NOs, Commercial $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ and CNTs) and LiPSs, using $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ as the representative LiPSs. A $0.02\mathbf{M}$ solution of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ in dimethoxyethane/dioxolane was prepared via the previously reported method [48]. In the visual LiPSs absorption ability test, equivalent amount $\left(50\mathrm{mg}\right)$ of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs, Commercial $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ and CNTs were added into ${\mathsf{5}}{\mathrm{mL}}$ of the yellow-brown $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ solution, different LiPSs absorption abilities of sulfur host materials were directly shown by the decoloration of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ solution. As shown in the inset photographs of Fig. 5a b, after adding the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs (bottle $\textcircled{2}$ ), the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ solution obviously faded within $5\mathrm{{min}}$ and became colourless after $30\mathrm{min}$ , indicating superior adsorption ability on LiPSs. In the meantime, the Commercial $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ soaking $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ solution (bottle $\textcircled{3}$ ) turned light yellow within $5\mathrm{min}$ , while insufficiently faded after $30\mathrm{min}$ , which could be attributed to the fact that the Commercial $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ bulks with smooth surface and large size lack sufficient LiPSs adsorption sites. Besides, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ solution with CNTs (bottle $\textcircled{4}$ ) showed little change in color within $30\mathrm{min}$ which could be ascribed to their relatively weak interaction with $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ . UV vis spectroscopy analysis was conducted to further quantitatively evaluate the concentration change in the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ solution. As showed in Fig. 5a b, the characteristic absorption band at the region of $280{-}290\mathrm{nm}$ can be ascribed to $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ in the solution [49]. In particular, the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ solution with the addition of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs shows weakest absorption band either after 5 min or $30\mathrm{min}$ soaking, suggesting the lowest residual concentration of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ in the solution, which is consistent with the visual LiPSs absorption ability test results. Overall, the strong LiPSs adsorption ability and abundant adsorption sites of the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs enable the effective entrapment of LiPSs, thereby resulting in adequate suppression on the shuttle effect during the charge/discharge processes.
The surface chemical interaction between LiPSs and the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs can be further revealed by the XPS analyses on the dry powder of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ and $\mathrm{\Delta\mathbf{Mo}_{2}C-C}$ NOs before/after the soaking process. As shown in Fig. 5c, three well-distinguished peaks at 167.1, 163.2 and $161.7\mathrm{eV}$ can be observed in the $\textsf{s}2\mathrm{p}$ spectra of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}.$ , which can be ascribed to the thiosulfate, bridging S $(\mathbb{S}_{\mathrm{~B}}^{0})$ and terminal S $(S^{-1}\mathrm{_T})$ , respectively [50].
Besides, the XPS spectra of Mo 3d for the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs are showed as Fig. 5d, two characteristic peaks at 232.9 and $228.5\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to the Mo O and Mo C bond, respectively. The Mo O bond may originate from the surface oxidation of $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ [51]. For comparison, the spectra of S 2p and Mo 3d show obvious changes after the soaking process. In particular, the S 2p spectra of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ after soaking the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs (Fig. 5e) shows a new peak at $161.3\mathrm{eV}$ , which be attributed to the formation of the Mo S bond [52]. Moreover, the peaks of thiosulfate, $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{~B~}}^{0}$ and $\mathbf{S}_{\mathrm{~\tiny~T~}}^{-1}$ represent a positive shift of 0.5, 0.3 and $0.7\mathrm{eV}$ to 167.6, 163.5 and $162.4\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, further confirming the chemical interaction between $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ and LiPSs. Furthermore, the corresponding Mo 3d spectra of the $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{6}}$ soaked $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs (Fig. 5f) exhibits an additional peak at $229.1\mathrm{eV}$ , proving the existence of the Mo S bond [27]. Meanwhile, the new peak at $226.5\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to the S 2s component. It is worth noting that the peaks of the Mo C and Mo O bond both show a $0.2\mathrm{eV}$ negative shift to lower binding energy, indicating the increased electron density of Mo atoms. This can be attributed to the deviation of electrons away from the electronegative S atoms of sulfur species to the electropositive Mo atoms of $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ , thus decreasing the binding energy of Mo while increasing the binding energy of S [53]. In brief, the XPS analysis results reveal strong chemical interaction between $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ and LiPSs via the formation of the Mo S bond, which are consistent with the results of the visual LiPSs absorption ability test and UV vis spectroscopy analysis.
## 3. Conclusions
In summary, we have prepared a unique $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs hybrid with polar $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ nanoparticles embedded in porous carbon matrices and used it as a multi-functional sulfur host for high-performance Li S batteries. Benefiting from the structure advantages, chemical adsorption ability and electrochemical catalytic effect, the as-prepared $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ C NOs greatly promote the electrochemical performances of Li S batteries. Specifically, $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ cathode exhibits a high initial specific capacity of $1050\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ and obtains an ultra-low average capacity decay rate of $0.0457\%$ per cycle within 600 cycles at $_{1\mathrm{~C~}}$ Furthermore, the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ $\mathsf{N O s@S}$ cathode with the high areal sulfur loading of $4.2\ \mathrm{mg}_{\mathrm{S}}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ delivers a high initial discharge specific capacity of $807\mathrm{~mAh~}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and maintains a capacity of $623\mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{~C~}$ . Overall, this work provides a promising strategy to design the multi-functional sulfur host for advanced Li S batteries with desirable specific capacity, outstanding cycling stability and high areal sulfur loading.
## 4. Experimental section
## 4.1. Synthesis of NENU-5 NOs
All chemicals and solvents were reagent grade and used as received. The NENU-5 NOs were synthesized according to the previously reported method [26]. In a typical process, $0.2{\mathrm g}$ of copper (II) acetate monohydrate (Sigma-Aldrich), certain amount of $\mathbf{L}$ -glutamic acid (0, 30, 60 or $120\mathrm{mg}$ , Sigma-Aldrich) and $0.3{\mathrm g}$ of phosphomolybdic acid hydrate (Sigma-Aldrich) were dissolved into $40\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, respectively, and stirred at ambient condition. After $10\mathrm{min}$ , $40\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol with $0.14g$ of 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid (Sigma-Aldrich) was poured into the above solution under vigorous stirring. The clear blues-green solution immediately turned light green suspension due to the rapid formation of NENU-5 nanoparticles. After stirring for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature, the light green precipitate was separated by centrifugation and washed three times with ethanol, followed by drying under vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . NENU-5 NOs prepared with $60\mathrm{mg}$ of L-glutamic acid were used in the following experiments, due to their regular octahedral shapes and nanometric sizes.
## 4.2. Synthesis of porous $M o_{2}C{-}C$ Nos
4.3. Synthesis of $M o_{2}C{\mathrm{-}}C N O s@S,$ commercial $M o_{2}C@S$ and CNTs@S composites
Sulfur encapsulation was conducted via a melt-diffusion process. In a typical synthesis, a mixture of sublimed sulfur (Sigma-Aldrich) and $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathrm{C-C}$ NOs with a weight ratio of 14:5 was hand-milled for $20\mathrm{min}$ and kept in a sealed vessel, followed by heating to $155^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ in an electric oven to afford the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ composite. The Commercial $\mathbf{Mo_{2}C}@S$ and $\mathrm{CNTs}@S$ composites were synthesized in the similar process, except that the sulfur host materials were replaced by the commercial molybdenum carbide (Shanghai Yunfu Nanotechnology Co., Ltd) and carbon nanotubes (CNTs, Sigma-Aldrich), respectively.
## 4.4. Visualized LiPSs adsorption test
A $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ solution $_{(0.02\mathrm{M})}$ was prepared by dissolving $55\mathrm{mg}$ lithium sulfide (Alfa Aesar) and $260\mathrm{mg}$ sublimed sulfur (Sigma-Aldrich) into $\mathbf{80mL}$ mixed solvent of dimethoxyethane/dioxolane (DME/DOL, V/ $\mathsf{V}=1{:}1$ ) under vigorous stirring in an Ar-filled glovebox with $<0.1$ ppm $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . In a typical visualized LiPSs adsorption test, $50\mathrm{mg}$
