## 4.7. Symmetric cell assembly and measurements
Electrochemical measurements were conducted by CR2016 coin-type test cells assembled in an argon-filled glovebox with $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ content less than $0.1\mathrm{ppm}$ , using lithium metal as anode and Celgard 2400 membrane as separator. Working electrodes involving different sulfur composites were prepared by ball-milling $80\%$ of the active materials $\mathrm{(Mo_{2}C-C}$ $\operatorname{NOs}@S$ , Commercial $\mathbf{Mo}_{2}\mathbf{C}@S$ and $\mathrm{CNTs}@S\mathcal{N}$ , $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ acetylene black, $10\mathrm{wt\%}$ water-soluble polymer n-lauryl acrylate (LA, Chengdu, China) and certain amount of deionized water into a homogeneous slurry. After that, the above slurry was evenly coated on the aluminum foils then dried at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight to afford the electrodes. General areal sulfur mass loading of the electrodes for the cyclic-voltammetry (CV) measurements, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) and long-term cycling performance tests at 1 C were about $1.1\mathrm{mgs}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathtt{-C}\mathtt{N O s}\ @\mathtt{S}$ cathodes with high areal sulfur mass loading of 2.1, 3.2 and $4.2~\mathrm{mgs}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ were prepared to evaluate their high-loading potential towards practical applications. Besides, the $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}\mathrm{-C}$ NOs, Commercial $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ and CNTs electrodes for the symmetric cells were synthesized in the similar process, except that the loading materials were replaced by the pure $\bf{M o}_{2}c\mathrm{-}\bf{C}\mathrm{NOs}$ , Commercial $\mathtt{M o_{2}C}$ and CNTs, respectively, and the collector is carbon paper (Toray, TGP-H120). The electrolyte was homemade by mixing $^{1\mathrm{M}}$ lithium bis-(trifluoromethanesulfonyl) imide (LiTFSI, TCI Japan) and $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{LiNO}_{3}$ (Sigma-Aldrich) with the solvent of DME and DOL $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathrm{V}/\mathrm{V}=1{:}1\dot{}}\end{array}$ . The electrolyte/sulfur ratio was kept at about 30:1 $\mathrm{(\upmuL\mg_{s}^{-1})}$ . A $0.5\ensuremath{\mathrm{~M~Li_{2}S_{6}~}}$ catholyte was prepared by adding $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ and sulfur powder (molar ratio $=1{:}5$ ) into the as prepared electrolyte and stirring at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$
Electrochemical measurements for the symmetric cell were conducted by CR2025 coin-type test cells assembled in an argon-filled glovebox with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ content less than $0.1\mathrm{ppm}$ , using Celgard $2400\ \mathrm{mem}\cdot$ brane as separator. Two as prepared circular electrodes of $\bf{M o}_{2}c\mathrm{-C}\mathrm{NOs}$ , Commercial $\mathtt{M o}_{2}\mathtt{C}$ or CNTs were used both as the working and counter electrodes with a total mass of about $2.4\mathrm{mg}$ . A $40\upmu\mathrm{L}0.5\mathrm{M}\mathrm{Li}_{2}S_{6}$ catholyte was used both as the electrolyte and active material. Then, the polarization curves were recorded with a CHI660E workstation within a voltage window of $-1.0$ to $1.0\mathrm{V}$ at scanning rate of $10\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ .
